-- Insert test data for a system where multiple sessions per day are possible (simulate a scenario where the primary key is not enforced or EVENT_DATE is a timestamp, so that duplicate dates for a given player can occur)

-- Player 100: First login on 2023-01-01, then two sessions on 2023-01-02
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (100, '2023-01-01', 10, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (100, '2023-01-02', 10, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (100, '2023-01-02', 11, 2);

-- Player 200: Single login on 2023-01-01 (no session on the next day)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (200, '2023-01-01', 20, 4);

/*
Q1 will perform a join between each player's first login (MIN_DATE) and all activity rows. In the case of PLAYER 100, the join returns three rows. The CASE expression will evaluate TRUE for both rows on 2023-01-02 (since 2023-01-01 + 1 = 2023-01-02) and add 1 for each, resulting in a subtotal of 2 for PLAYER 100. For PLAYER 200, there is no next‐day record so the contribution is 0. The denominator (COUNT(DISTINCT PLAYER_ID)) is 2, giving Q1 a fraction of 2/2 = 1.00.

In contrast, Q2 uses a subquery that counts each player at most once if they have any login on the day after their first login. Thus, PLAYER 100 will contribute 1 and PLAYER 200 0. With a denominator of 2, Q2 computes the fraction as 1/2 = 0.50.

This difference in aggregation leads to Q1 and Q2 returning different results.
*/

