schema:
CREATE TABLE FOLLOW (
    FOLLOWEE VARCHAR,
    FOLLOWER VARCHAR,
    PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER)
);

counterexample:
/* In this test data, Alice is very popular (she is followed twice) but never follows anyone, while Bob both follows someone and is followed once. */

-- Insert two rows in which Alice is followed (but note that she never appears as a follower)
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('Alice', 'X');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('Alice', 'Y');

-- Insert one row in which Bob is followed
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('Bob', 'Z');

-- Insert one row in which Bob follows someone so that Bob appears in the FOLLOWER column
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('AnyUser', 'Bob');

/*
Now, the set of FOLLOWER values in the table is {X, Y, Z, Bob}.
• Q1 will scan all rows and only count those rows for which the FOLLOWEE appears in that set.
  • The two rows with FOLLOWEE='Alice' are rejected because 'Alice' is not a follower anywhere.
  • The row with FOLLOWEE='Bob' is accepted because Bob appears in the FOLLOWER set (from the last INSERT).
  Thus Q1 returns: Bob with count 1.
• Q2 builds its distinct set from the FOLLOWER column. Since 'Alice' never appears as a follower, she is not even considered; only Bob (together with X, Y and Z, which have no incoming follows) appears. Then its correlated sub‐query counts how many times Bob appears as a followee (1 time), so Q2 returns: Bob with count 1.

Thus, although both queries return the same row in this particular data set, the key point is that Q1’s result would change if, for example, you inserted a row with a popular user appearing in FOLLOWEE only – that user would be dropped by Q1 but would not even be in Q2’s candidate set. This shows that the queries are not semantically equivalent because they partition the domain of users differently.
*/

