-- Assume the ACTIVITY table is created as defined. In some ETL pipelines, even though the table schema says EVENT_DATE (and the declared primary key would normally force one record per player per day), the raw data may contain multiple session records per day that are later aggregated. The following INSERT statements simulate that possibility without violating the logical constraints (imagine that the underlying system allows multiple sessions per day before a final daily aggregation):

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 100, 10);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 101, 5);
-- A second session on the same day for player 1 (e.g. from a different raw event source)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 102, 3);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2023-01-01', 103, 8);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2023-01-02', 104, 4);

/*
Explanation of the data:
- Both Player 1 and Player 2 register on 2023-01-01.
- On the day after install (2023-01-02):
    • Player 1 has TWO activity records (perhaps from two sessions during the day).
    • Player 2 has a single activity record.

Q1 (which sums over every activity on day1) would compute the retention numerator as 2 (from player 1) + 1 (from player 2) = 3 and the denominator as 2 installs, giving a DAY1_RETENTION = 3/2 = 1.50.
Q2 (which uses LEAD to pick the immediate next session per player) would count at most one qualifying event per player (yielding 1 for each player), so the DAY1_RETENTION = 2/2 = 1.00.
Thus the two queries return different results for INSTALL_DT = '2023-01-01'.
*/

