-- Then suppose these queries are used in a larger query such as:
--
-- SELECT Q1.REPORT_REASON, Q1.REPORT_COUNT, X.POST_ID AS outer_post_id
-- FROM ( SELECT EXTRA AS REPORT_REASON, COUNT(DISTINCT POST_ID) AS REPORT_COUNT FROM ACTIONS 
--        WHERE ACTION = 'REPORT' AND ACTION_DATE = '2019-07-04' GROUP BY EXTRA ) Q1
-- CROSS JOIN ( SELECT 999 AS POST_ID ) X;

-- In such a construct the unqualified POST_ID in Q1 may accidentally be bound to X.POST_ID (i.e. 999), so that COUNT(DISTINCT POST_ID) becomes 1 for every group. By contrast, if Q2 were used in place of Q1 then COUNT(DISTINCT A.POST_ID) will correctly count the distinct post ids from ACTIONS. This demonstrates that the two queries are not semantically equivalent in a context where an outer POST_ID is in scope.

