/*
Assume that despite the primary key on (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE), the business logic allows a player to get two records on the same day if the DEVICE_ID differs. (For this counterexample, imagine that the key constraint is defined on (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) in the schema but the application sometimes inserts duplicate EVENT_DATE rows when the DEVICE_ID is different.)

The following INSERT statements insert 2 players. Player 1 gets two records on 2021-01-02 (the day after his first activity on 2021-01-01) while player 2 gets one record on 2021-01-02 following his 2021-01-01 activity. 

Under Q1 the sum over players will be 1 (for player 1) + 1 (for player 2) = 2, and dividing by 2 players gives a fraction of 1.00. 
Under Q2 the COUNT(C.PLAYER_ID) will be 2 (from player 1) + 1 (from player 2) = 3 while the denominator remains 2, so the fraction becomes 1.50. 
*/

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 101, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 102, 4);

