schema:
CREATE TABLE FOLLOW (
    FOLLOWEE VARCHAR,
    FOLLOWER VARCHAR,
    PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER)
);

counterexample:
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A','B');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A','C');
-- Here A is followed two times

-- In addition, add one row where A is in the follower role (so that A qualifies in the WHERE of Q1)
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('D','A');

-- Now add one more follow record that makes the picture ambiguous on the join side: suppose someone follows A (again) but with the same follower as an earlier row on the A–side
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A','B');

-- Note: The last INSERT would normally be rejected because (A,B) is already present (the primary key is on (FOLLOWEE,FOLLOWER)). To simulate “join‐duplication” without violating the primary key constraint we instead insert a slightly different row that produces the same join effect. For that purpose we use a dummy user X who follows A and then A is followed by the same user B.

INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('X','A');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A','B_dummy');

-- With these rows (and remembering that the subquery in Q1 uses all values in FOLLOWER) the two queries will not be equivalent. Q1, grouping by FOLLOWEE, will count all rows where the candidate appears as a followee provided the candidate appears somewhere as follower. In contrast, Q2’s join (which implicitly “starts” its grouping from the row(s) where the candidate is a follower) will only report join partners coming from the one row for A (namely the single row in which A appears as follower among the X row) and so its count over the joined rows (even after DISTINCT) will differ from Q1’s raw count.

