schema:
CREATE TABLE CINEMA (
    SEAT_ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FREE BOOL
);

counterexample:
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO CINEMA (SEAT_ID, FREE) VALUES (4, 1);

-- Explanation of the data:
-- Seat 1 is free and has seat 2 (free) as its right neighbor, so Q1 returns 1.
-- Seat 2 is free and has seat 1 (free) as its left neighbor, so Q1 returns 2.
-- Seat 4 is free but its adjacent seat (seat 3 or seat 5) is missing, so Q1 does not return 4.
-- In Q2 only seats that successfully join with an adjacent free seat appear (here only seats 1 and 2 join); seat 4 has no partner and is absent.

-- If one were to alter the data (for example, by adding a free row for seat 5) then Q1 would count seat 4 (which has seat 5 free) while Q2 would only report seat 4 if it appears as the first member of the free adjacent pair. Hence, by using sparse seat numbers the two queries can be distinguished.

