/*
Interpretation:
  • Q1 (which joins all PRODUCT and SALES rows) will see that Buyer 2 has an iPhone sale and so exclude Buyer 2, returning Buyer 1 and Buyer 3.
  • Q2’s CTE only extracts rows for products named exactly 'S8' or 'IPHONE'. This drops Buyer 1’s GALAXY sale. Then the outer query excludes any buyer appearing in the 'IPHONE' part of the CTE. Consequently, Buyer 2 is removed and Buyer 1 and Buyer 3 remain.

However, if later one joins back to the full SALES table (thereby “recovering” Buyer 1’s extraneous GALAXY sale) then Q1 would have effectively used that extra evidence to differentiate the buyers, whereas Q2 would not. This difference in how the complete SALES data is brought into play can result in differing results in an application that relies on additional SALES details.*/

