-- Assuming our SQL engine uses an undisclosed tie-breaker for rows with the same EVENT_DATE, the following inserts illustrate the idea:

-- Player 1: installs on 2021-01-01 and then logs in on 2021-01-02 (the immediate next login)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 101, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);

-- Player 2: installs on 2021-01-01 but has two activities on the next day. One of them (due to a tie-breaker) will be chosen by LEAD as the immediate next login, but it might not be the one having the day+1 property if the tie-breaker orders it later.
-- To simulate this, we assume that a hidden time component (not visible in the DATE type) allows two records on 2021-01-02 for the same player.
-- (In a real system the PK might allow multiple rows if EVENT_DATE were a DATETIME type.)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-01', 202, 10);
-- Two different logins on 2021-01-02 (which would be permitted if EVENT_DATE were DATETIME; here we show the idea even though the DATE column forces uniqueness)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 202, 4);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 203, 7);

-- Player 2 also logs in on 2021-01-03.
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-03', 202, 8);

-- Explanation of impact:
-- Q1 will join all of player 2's activities and, when grouping by the install date (2021-01-01), it will find two records on 2021-01-02 (matching install+1) so the SUM will be 2 (even though the COUNT of distinct players is 1), leading to a day1 retention ratio of 2/1 = 2.00 (which would later be rounded to 2.00).
-- In contrast, Q2 uses LEAD and sees only one immediate next login for player 2 (depending on the internal ordering) so it will count player 2 only once as retained, yielding a day1 retention ratio of 1/1 = 1.00 for that player.
-- As a result, when aggregating across players who installed on 2021-01-01, Q1 and Q2 return different DAY1_RETENTION values.

-- Note: In our test case the potential for duplicate day+1 rows is only possible if the date column were of higher resolution (e.g. DATETIME) or the PK were defined differently. This counterexample therefore illustrates the conceptual difference in logic between the two approaches.

