counterexample:
-- Initial test data

INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (2);

-- Customer 1 bought both products
INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1, 2);

/*
At this point:
  • (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) FROM PRODUCT) returns 2
  • For customer 1, COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) is 2, so both Q1 and Q2 return customer 1.

Simulate a concurrent update (or a change in snapshot) by inserting a new product.
Assume that Q2’s CTE is evaluated before the new product is added, while Q1’s subquery is evaluated after the insertion.
*/

-- A new product is added later
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (3);

/*
Now:
  • Q1: (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) FROM PRODUCT) returns 3, so customer 1 (whose count is still 2) no longer qualifies.
  • Q2: If its CTE was computed (and materialized) before the new product was inserted so that CNT remains 2, customer 1 still qualifies.

Thus the two queries yield different results in this counterexample scenario.
*/

