   In Q1 the subquery uses an inner join so the sale with PRODUCT_ID = 2 is dropped, and buyer 1 is kept since buyer 1 never appears in the subquery.
   In Q2 the LEFT JOIN in the subquery produces a row (with a NULL for PRODUCT_NAME) for that dangling iPhone sale. Because the WHERE clause
   (P.PRODUCT_NAME = 'IPHONE') fails, the subquery nevertheless returns a row containing a NULL, causing the NOT IN predicate to fail for all.
   (Thus Q1 returns buyer 1 but Q2 returns no rows.) */
