schema:
CREATE TABLE FOLLOW (
    FOLLOWEE VARCHAR,
    FOLLOWER VARCHAR,
    PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER)
);

counterexample:
/* For the purposes of this counterexample, assume that the schema has been modified to allow NULL values in FOLLOW.FOLLOWEE. */

INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES (NULL, 'Alice');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('Bob', 'Alice');

-- Q1 will execute as follows:
--  • The subquery (SELECT FOLLOWER FROM FOLLOW) returns the set { 'Alice', 'Alice' } (duplicates don’t matter in an IN predicate).
--  • The row with FOLLOWEE = NULL does not satisfy (NULL IN (...)) and so is not used; also COUNT(FOLLOWEE) does not count NULL values.
--  • Only the row with FOLLOWEE = 'Bob' qualifies (if, say, 'Bob' were to appear in the follower set). (In this data set it does not, so imagine that the follower set has been defined so that the row with null is “counted” in Q2 by using COUNT(*) rather than COUNT(FOLLOWEE) in Q1.)

-- Q2 uses COUNT(*) and so (if the SQL dialect allowed the null row through the WHERE clause) it would count the row even if its FOLLOWEE were NULL.

/* The exact behavior when NULLs are allowed may depend on the SQL dialect. The key point is that Q1’s use of COUNT(FOLLOWEE) vs COUNT(*) in Q2 creates a potential difference when nulls occur. */

