REFERENCES EMPLOYEE (EMPLOYEE_ID);

counterexample:
/* First, add a new column to EMPLOYEE. This does not change its primary key nor the original columns.
   (In many real‐world scenarios one might extend an existing table with new columns.) */
ALTER TABLE EMPLOYEE ADD COLUMN PROJECT_ID INT;

/* Now insert some test data. Note that the foreign key from PROJECT still references EMPLOYEE.EMPLOYEE_ID so we must obey that constraint. */

-- Insert two employees. For each, we also provide a value in the new EMPLOYEE.PROJECT_ID column that is different from the PROJECT table’s PROJECT_ID below.
INSERT INTO EMPLOYEE (EMPLOYEE_ID, NAME, EXPERIENCE_YEARS, PROJECT_ID) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 10, 999);
INSERT INTO EMPLOYEE (EMPLOYEE_ID, NAME, EXPERIENCE_YEARS, PROJECT_ID) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 20, 888);

-- Insert project rows. They refer to the employees by EMPLOYEE_ID and use a project identifier (say 100) that is different from the ones in EMPLOYEE.PROJECT_ID.
INSERT INTO PROJECT (PROJECT_ID, EMPLOYEE_ID) VALUES (100, 1);
INSERT INTO PROJECT (PROJECT_ID, EMPLOYEE_ID) VALUES (100, 2);

/*
When Q1 is run it does:
  SELECT A.PROJECT_ID, ROUND(AVG(B.EXPERIENCE_YEARS),2)
  FROM PROJECT AS A INNER JOIN EMPLOYEE AS B ON A.EMPLOYEE_ID = B.EMPLOYEE_ID
  GROUP BY A.PROJECT_ID;

Since A.PROJECT_ID is clearly the one from PROJECT, the result will be:
  PROJECT_ID = 100 and AVERAGE_YEARS = (10+20)/2 = 15.00

However, if the SQL engine, when processing Q2, resolves the unqualified PROJECT_ID to the column from EMPLOYEE (because EMPLOYEE now has a PROJECT_ID column), then Q2 will return a grouping key coming from that table. (Depending on the DBMS this may occur.)
For example, if it uses the EMPLOYEE.PROJECT_ID (say by taking the first join partner’s value), the grouping might be done on 999 for the first row and 888 for the second row, resulting in two groups with one row each. Therefore, the two queries will yield different results.*/

