counterexample:
/* Insert test data. Note: To run this counterexample the primary key constraint on PRODUCT must be disabled so that duplicates can be inserted, because otherwise the duplicate row would be rejected. */

-- Insert products: product 1 and product 2. Then insert a duplicate row for product 2.
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (2);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (2);  -- duplicate row, which makes COUNT(*) return 3 but COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) returns 2

-- Insert a customer who has purchased product 1 and product 2
INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO CUSTOMER (CUSTOMER_ID, PRODUCT_KEY) VALUES (1, 2);

/*
Now, executing Q1:
  SELECT CUSTOMER_ID
  FROM CUSTOMER
  GROUP BY CUSTOMER_ID
  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) FROM PRODUCT);

The subquery (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) FROM PRODUCT) returns 2 (since the distinct keys are {1,2}). For CUSTOMER 1, COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) is 2, so customer 1 is returned.

Executing Q2:
  WITH PURCHASED AS (
    SELECT CUSTOMER_ID AS ID, COUNT(DISTINCT PRODUCT_KEY) AS PURCHASES
    FROM CUSTOMER
    GROUP BY CUSTOMER_ID
  )
  SELECT ID AS CUSTOMER_ID
  FROM PURCHASED
  WHERE PURCHASES = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM PRODUCT);

The subquery (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM PRODUCT) returns 3 because there are 3 product rows. For CUSTOMER 1, PURCHASES is 2, so the equality fails and no customer is returned.

Thus the two queries produce different results with the above data.
*/

