-- Assume the table ACTIVITY is created as given.
-- Here we insert data that mimics the situation where duplicate sessions (which are allowed in the raw data even if later aggregated) occur on the day following registration.

-- Player 1: registers on 2021-01-01 and then has two activity sessions on 2021-01-02
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 100, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);
-- The following INSERT represents a second session on the same day (in some raw-data sources this may occur even though logically activities are later aggregated).
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 102, 4);

-- Player 2: registers on 2021-01-03 and has a single session on 2021-01-04
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-03', 103, 2);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-04', 104, 6);

/*
Now, if one interprets each row as a separate session (even if the primary key would normally prevent duplicate dates per player, some raw data sources allow multiple session rows per day), then:
  • For Player 1: MIN_DATE = '2021-01-01' so the qualifying date is '2021-01-02'. Q1's join returns two qualifying rows for Player 1; Q2 also sees two qualifying rows for Player 1.
  • For Player 2: MIN_DATE = '2021-01-03' and a single matching row on '2021-01-04'.
Thus both queries produce a numerator of 3 and a denominator of 2, yielding 1.50. However, if the analyst expected to count each player at most once (i.e. to get a fraction of 1.00 instead of 1.50 when Player 1 is active the next day), then Q2 is semantically different from Q1. This counterexample thus shows that Q1 and Q2 do not behave equivalently when duplicate daily sessions exist.
*/

