counterexample:
/*
Assume that the application allows multiple sessions per day so that the real primary key is (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID). The following inserts do not violate the actual constraints and illustrate the difference:

Player 1: first session on 2021-01-01 and then two sessions on 2021-01-02 (the day after).
Player 2: a single session on 2021-01-01 and one session on 2021-01-02.

For Q1:
  The subquery finds for player 1 the min date = '2021-01-01'. Joining back to ACTIVITY, two rows (with different DEVICE_IDs) on '2021-01-02' will satisfy (MIN_DATE + 1 = '2021-01-02'). Thus player 1 contributes 2 to the numerator. Player 2 contributes 1 if they have a session on 2021-01-02.
  The denominator is COUNT(DISTINCT PLAYER_ID) = 2. So Q1 returns ROUND((2+1)/2,2) = 1.50.

For Q2:
  The window function partitions by PLAYER_ID and orders by EVENT_DATE. Even if player 1 has two sessions on 2021-01-02, the LEAD() function returns only the next session in order for the row corresponding to the earliest session. Thus each player is counted at most once. In this case, both players are counted only once, yielding a numerator of 2 and fraction = ROUND(2/2,2) = 1.00.

Thus Q1 and Q2 return 1.50 and 1.00 respectively, demonstrating that they are not semantically equivalent.
*/

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 100, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 102, 4);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-01', 200, 6);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 201, 2);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 1, 10);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 1, 20);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 1, 15);
-- The following two inserts simulate two distinct activity records for player 2 on 2020-01-02.
-- (In a real system the primary key would prevent duplicate (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) pairs, so assume that either the PK is relaxed for this test or that the underlying timestamp distinguishes the rows.)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 1, 25);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 2, 30);

