counterexample:
-- Assume the following schema has already been created and the constraints are enforced

-- Insert products
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (1, 'S8', 400);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 1000);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (3, 'CASE', 20);

-- Insert sales
-- Buyer 1 has three sales: one S8 sale, one IPHONE sale, and one CASE sale
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 101, 1, '2023-10-01', 1, 400);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (2, 102, 1, '2023-10-02', 1, 1000);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (3, 103, 1, '2023-10-03', 1, 20);

-- Buyer 2 has only an S8 sale
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 104, 2, '2023-10-04', 1, 400);

/*
Under these circumstances:
  • Q1 works as follows:
    - It only considers the S8 sale rows (so it sees Buyer 1 and Buyer 2 from the S8 join).
    - Then its subquery (which uses an inner join) will detect that Buyer 1 made an IPHONE purchase and so exclude Buyer 1. Only Buyer 2 remains.
  • Q2 first builds the S8 CTE (listing Buyer 1 and Buyer 2) and the IPHONE CTE (listing Buyer 1). But then the final SELECT scans the entire SALES table. For Buyer 1 one of the rows (e.g. the CASE sale) does not “carry” the fact that his S8 row is disqualified when looked at in isolation, so Buyer 1 may erroneously survive the row‐by‐row filter. In other words, because Q2 is applied over all SALES rows (not just those whose product is S8) the filtering “leaks” Buyer 1 into the final result.
Thus Q1 returns only Buyer 2 while Q2 returns both Buyer 1 and Buyer 2, demonstrating that the two queries aren’t equivalent.
*/

