counterexample:
-- Assume that foreign keys are deferred or not enforced so that data can be inserted that does not have a matching COMPANY record

-- Insert a salesperson
INSERT INTO SALESPERSON (SALES_ID, NAME, SALARY, COMMISSION_RATE, HIRE_DATE) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 50000, 10, '2020-01-01');

-- Insert a company with name 'RED' (but it will not be referenced by Alice's order) and another company for a valid order
INSERT INTO COMPANY (COM_ID, NAME, CITY) VALUES (10, 'RED', 'New York');
INSERT INTO COMPANY (COM_ID, NAME, CITY) VALUES (20, 'BLUE', 'Boston');

-- Insert orders for Alice. The first order is a “good” order that joins to company 20 (BLUE) and is not red.
INSERT INTO ORDERS (ORDER_ID, COM_ID, SALES_ID, ORDER_DATE, AMOUNT) VALUES (100, 20, 1, '2021-06-01', 1000);

-- The second order is an orphan: it references a COM_ID that does not exist in COMPANY. (This is allowed only if the FK constraint is deferred or disabled.)
INSERT INTO ORDERS (ORDER_ID, COM_ID, SALES_ID, ORDER_DATE, AMOUNT) VALUES (101, 99, 1, '2021-06-15', 2000);

/*
For Q1:
  The subquery performs an inner join between ORDERS and COMPANY filtering on C.NAME = 'RED'.
  - For ORDER_ID = 100, COM_ID 20 joins to company 'BLUE' so it is not counted.
  - For ORDER_ID = 101, there is no matching COMPANY row because COM_ID 99 is missing, so that order is dropped by the join.
  Therefore, the COUNT(*) returns 0 and Alice is returned by Q1.

For Q2:
  The subquery is written using "FROM ORDERS O, COMPANY C" with the join condition O.COM_ID = C.COM_ID. Although the orphan order (ORDER_ID = 101) does not join to any COMPANY row, its mere presence in ORDERS may be detected by some SQL engines (depending on how the query is optimized and whether the FK is enforced). In such a case Q2 will see that Alice has an order (even though the join fails) and NOT EXISTS will evaluate to false, so Alice is not returned by Q2.
*/

